---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'About the project | teleprompter.wtf';
const description = 'Why teleprompter.wtf is free, private, open-source, and intentionally simple.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose-page section-wrap">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the project</p>
      <h1>A teleprompter should not need onboarding.</h1>
      <p>
        teleprompter.wtf is a small, open-source utility built around one idea: paste your words and
        start presenting.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <div class="prose-aside"><p>Paste.<br />Present.<br />Done.</p></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2>Why this exists</h2>
        <p>
          Many teleprompters begin with account creation, pricing decisions, or feature-heavy
          dashboards. Those things get in the way of the moment when you simply need readable words
          moving at the right pace.
        </p>
        <p>
          This project treats speed, typography, privacy, accessibility, and predictable controls as
          the product, not as premium extras.
        </p>
        <h2>Open source on purpose</h2>
        <p>
          The code is available so anyone can inspect how local storage and analytics work, report
          an issue, improve accessibility, or self-host the tool. The core application does not need
          a database or a script-storage service.
        </p>
        <p>
          Local voice tracking is documented in detail, including the pinned model, temporary
          recognition state, and fallback behavior. <a href="/private-voice-tracking"
            >Read how it works</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/SABYA648/Telemprompter-WTF" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >View SABYA648/Telemprompter-WTF on GitHub</a
          >.
        </p>
        <h2>What it will not do</h2>
        <p>
          teleprompter.wtf will not put scripts in public links, require an account for the basic
          presenter, sell attention to advertisers, or claim your words need a cloud database to
          scroll on your screen.
        </p>
        <h2>Use it, then tell us what hurt</h2>
        <p>
          The most useful contributions are concrete: a browser where fullscreen behaves oddly, a
          control that is hard to reach on a particular phone, or a wording choice that does not
          work with assistive technology. Open a GitHub issue with reproducible details. Never
          include a private script.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
